course: dictionary
lesson: 3897
title: Friction: when things rub
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Friction is a force that slows things down or stops them when two surfaces rub or slide against each other. A force is a push or a pull. «Friction acts between surfaces that rub and works against the movement.» So friction always pushes back against movement.
explain: Think about rubbing your hands together. «Rubbing your hands together makes them warm because friction turns motion into heat.» The rubbing is the movement, and the warmth is the heat that friction makes.
explain: The word has a second meaning too. When people have friction between them, they disagree or argue and do not get on well. Check the words around it to see which meaning fits: a surface, or people?
fact: Friction is a force that works against movement.
fact: «Rough surfaces make more friction than smooth surfaces.» So sandpaper grips more than glass does.
fact: Friction can also mean arguing between people, as in «friction between them».
fact: Friction makes heat, but it also wears things out, so oil and wheels reduce it.
fact: «Ball bearings» are tiny steel balls inside a wheel's hub, and they let the wheel turn with very little friction.
exercise: «Rough surfaces make more friction than smooth surfaces.» Which surface makes more friction? | A polished floor | *A rough, sandy path | A smooth, icy path | A wet glass table
exercise: «Rubbing your hands together makes them warm.» What makes them warm? | *Friction, which turns motion into heat | Sunlight | Soap | Cold air
exercise: Maya says, «There is friction between my brother and me.» What does friction mean here? | *They argue or do not get on | Sliding on ice | Rubbing their hands together
test: Why does ice make you slip? | Ice makes you lighter | *Ice has little friction, so your feet slide | Ice is too rough, so your feet stick | Ice makes heat, which melts your shoes
test: «Ball bearings let the wheel turn with very little friction.» Why are ball bearings used in wheels? | To make the wheel warm | *To let the wheel turn easily | To make more friction | To stop the wheel from moving
exam: Which use of friction is about surfaces, not people? | Friction makes arguments worse | *Shoe grips use friction to stop us slipping | The friction in our family is growing | Friction between them means they don't get on
